# Indian States and Union Territories: A Map That Keeps Redrawing Itself

> India's internal map is not fixed; it is a work in progress. The
> boundaries drawn in 1956 on linguistic lines have been redrawn more
> than a dozen times since — most recently in 2019, when Jammu and
> Kashmir became two union territories. Questions about states, their
> capitals, and their superlatives are guaranteed marks in every
> general-awareness paper, provided you memorise the *current* map and
> not an old one.

## 1. The Numbers: 28 States, 8 Union Territories

[definition]
As of 26 January 2020, India has **28 states** and **8 union
territories**. The count changed in 2019 (Jammu and Kashmir became two
UTs — J&K with a legislature, Ladakh without one) and again on 26
January 2020, when **Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu** were
merged into a single UT.
[/definition]

The current eight union territories: **Andaman and Nicobar Islands,
Chandigarh, Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu, Delhi (NCT),
Jammu and Kashmir, Ladakh, Lakshadweep, Puducherry**. Delhi and
Puducherry have legislatures; the rest are administered directly
through the centre.

| Superlative | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Largest state by area | Rajasthan (342,239 sq km) |
| Smallest state by area | Goa (3,702 sq km) |
| Most populous state | Uttar Pradesh |
| Least populous state | Sikkim |
| Largest UT by area | Jammu and Kashmir (after 2019) |
| Southernmost point of India | Indira Point, Great Nicobar |
| Northernmost point (administered) | Indira Col (Siachen area, Ladakh) |

## 2. How the Map Got This Way: 1956, 1960, 2000, 2019

The **States Reorganisation Act, 1956** redrew provincial boundaries
on linguistic lines, creating 14 states and 6 union territories. The
first state actually created on a linguistic basis came earlier, in
1953, when **Andhra State** was carved out of Madras after Potti
Sriramulu's fast-unto-death — a fact papers pair with the 1956 act to
catch the careless.

| Year | Change |
| --- | --- |
| 1953 | Andhra State split from Madras (first linguistic state) |
| 1956 | States Reorganisation Act — 14 states, 6 UTs |
| 1960 | Bombay split into Maharashtra and Gujarat |
| 1966 | Haryana carved from Punjab; Chandigarh made a shared UT |
| 1972 | Manipur, Meghalaya, Tripura, (Mizoram later, 1987) attain statehood |
| 2000 | Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand, Jharkhand created (Nov 2000) |
| 2014 | Telangana (29th state) split from Andhra Pradesh |
| 2019 | Jammu and Kashmir reorganised into two UTs |
| 2020 | Dadra & Nagar Haveli merged with Daman & Diu |

Note the trap in the 2000 row: three states were born the same
**year** but on different **dates** — Chhattisgarh (1 November),
Uttarakhand (9 November), and Jharkhand (15 November). When a paper
asks "which was the first state of November 2000," the answer is
Chhattisgarh.

## 3. Capitals Worth a Second Look

Most state capitals are famous cities, but a handful of papers adore
because the capital is *not* the largest city:

| State | Capital | The exam point |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Maharashtra | Mumbai | — |
| Kerala | Thiruvananthapuram | Formerly Trivandrum |
| Madhya Pradesh | Bhopal | Indore is the largest city |
| Rajasthan | Jaipur | — |
| Karnataka | Bengaluru | — |
| Tamil Nadu | Chennai | — |
| West Bengal | Kolkata | — |
| Nagaland | Kohima | Dimapur is the largest city |
| Meghalaya | Shillong | — |
| Assam | Dispur | A locality of Guwahati |
| Andhra Pradesh (post-2017) | Amaravati (proposed) / Visakhapatnam (announced seat) | The state has no settled single capital — a favourite moving-target question |
| Gujarat | Gandhinagar | Not Ahmedabad |

Two states have **two** legislative capitals by convention: **Himachal
Pradesh** (Shimla in summer, Dharamshala's winter session) — and
historically **Jammu and Kashmir** (Srinagar in summer, Jammu in
winter). Maharashtra's winter session moves to Nagpur. These seasonal
arrangements show up in state-level papers every year.

## 4. Statehood Days and Formation Trivia

Every state celebrates a foundation day, and national papers usually
ask for the cluster: Rajasthan Day (30 March), Maharashtra and
Gujarat Day (1 May), Kerala Day (1 November, coinciding with
Karnataka, Haryana, Punjab, MP and Chhattisgarh). The largest single
cluster — six states — shares **1 November**, a leftover of the 1956
reorganisation date. **1 May** (Maharashtra–Gujarat) and **1
November** (the "Linguistic Reorganisation Day" cluster) are the two
dates most worth remembering.

India's newest state, Telangana (2 June 2014), has Hyderabad as its
capital — a city that served as the joint capital of both Telugu
states for ten years. Its formation made the map 29 states strong
before the 2019–20 UT changes reshuffled the other column.

[example]
A standard trap sequence: (1) "How many states does India have?" — 28,
not 29, because J&K became a UT in 2019. (2) "Which is the smallest
state?" — Goa, not Sikkim (Sikkim is the least *populous*). (3)
"Which UT was created most recently?" — Ladakh and J&K in 2019, with
the 2020 *merger* reducing the UT count to eight. Read the
superlative in the question twice before marking the option.
[/example]

[summary]
Today's India is 28 states and 8 union territories, the map's latest
shake-ups being Telangana (2014), the bifurcation of Jammu and Kashmir
into two UTs (2019), and the merger of Dadra and Nagar Haveli with
Daman and Diu (2020). The 1956 States Reorganisation Act created the
linguistic map, but the first linguistic state was Andhra in 1953.
Rajasthan is the largest state, Goa the smallest, Uttar Pradesh the
most populous, Sikkim the least; Indira Point is the southernmost
tip. Watch for capitals that are not the largest city — Bhopal,
Dispur, Kohima, Gandhinagar — and for the trap between "number of
states" before and after 2019.
[/summary]
